WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.520
If it were not for Johanna van-Gogh Bonger, the&nbsp;
world would never have known The Starry Night.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.680
Or even heard of Vincent van Gogh, or rather,

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:12.440
van “gogh”, though I’m definitely&nbsp;
butchering the Dutch pronunciation.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:17.400
While Vincent was alive, hardly anyone knew&nbsp;
his work beyond a small circle of artists.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.400
Tired of the hustle and bustle of Paris,&nbsp;
where he tried to make a name for himself,

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.480
he left for the south of France where he&nbsp;
dreamt of building an artist’s community

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:30.000
at his home in Arles and coaxed fellow&nbsp;
painter Paul Gauguin to live with him.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.440
Gauguin didn’t want to be&nbsp;
in Arles, calling it “the

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:36.200
dirtiest hole in the South”.
Vincent’s dreams were dashed.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.320
Two days before Christmas in&nbsp;
1888, they had a huge fight.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.440
And in a fit of rage, Vincent turned&nbsp;
on himself and cut off his left ear.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.920
We don’t the exact nature&nbsp;
of Vincent’s mental illness,

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.760
but doctors and historians today believe&nbsp;
he may have suffered from bipolar disorder.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:57.720
When he voluntarily checked himself into a&nbsp;
psychiatric hospital in Saint-Rémy-de-Provence,

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.920
painting seemed to calm him,&nbsp;
but his deep anguish remained.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.000
Two months after leaving the hospital,&nbsp;
he shot himself in the chest.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.040
His brother Théo rushed to be by his&nbsp;
side, where he died on July 29, 1890.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.480
Vincent van Gogh was just 37 years old.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:20.720
As Théo put it in a letter to their&nbsp;
mother: “Life weighed so heavily on him.”

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.920
Vincent’s death also weighed&nbsp;
heavily on his brother.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:29.920
Théo’s already weakened health deteriorated,&nbsp;
and six months after Vincent took his own life,

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.600
Théo died of the effects of&nbsp;
syphilis. He was 33 years old.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.720
The medical file noted that the&nbsp;
cause of Théo’s decline was:

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:43.480
“heredity, chronic illness,&nbsp;
excessive strain and grief.”

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:48.320
Théo left behind an infant&nbsp;
son and a devastated wife.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.040
When Johanna, or Jo as she was commonly called,&nbsp;
married Théo, a new life opened up for her.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.760
The Dutch woman was whisked away to Paris, the&nbsp;
center of the art world, where Théo worked as an

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:02.640
art dealer, tirelessly promoting his brother’s&nbsp;
extensive collection of nearly 900 paintings.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.520
Théo spent his life trying to raise awareness&nbsp;
of his brother’s work but struggled.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.440
Van Gogh’s post-Impressionism style,&nbsp;
characterized by loose brushwork,

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.600
even bolder colors than Impressionism, and&nbsp;
great emotional depth, was in stark contrast to

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:22.800
traditional art, which emphasized finely detailed&nbsp;
brushwork, precise lines, and a polished finish.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.640
Despite the lack of enthusiasm for Vincent's&nbsp;
art, Théo believed in his brother’s genius,

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:31.160
writing to Jo: “...one day he will be understood.”

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:36.560
After Théo’s death, Jo made it her mission for&nbsp;
the world to understand and appreciate Vincent

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:43.160
van Gogh’s art, declaring, “It is the only thing&nbsp;
I can do in memory of my husband and for Vincent.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.360
This is the story of how she&nbsp;
made Vincent van Gogh famous.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:53.160
When Théo died unexpectedly two years after&nbsp;
marrying Jo, she left Paris and moved to Bussum,

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.960
southeast of Amsterdam, a village home&nbsp;
to writers, artists, and intellectuals.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.240
Because Théo didn’t leave a will, half of&nbsp;
Vincent's hundreds of works went to Jo,

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.360
and the other half to her son&nbsp;
Vincent, named after his uncle.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.680
Van Gogh dedicated Almond Blossom to his nephew.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:12.480
Jo looked after the boy’s share for&nbsp;
the time being since he wasn’t of age.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.720
She converted her large home into&nbsp;
a boarding house and stored many of

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:19.320
Vincent’s paintings that didn’t&nbsp;
fit on her walls in the attic.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.280
She then hatched a plan to&nbsp;
achieve recognition for Vincent.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.240
As described in the book about&nbsp;
her life by Hans Luijten,

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.840
“...Jo began knocking on doors&nbsp;
like a traveling salesman.”

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.920
She tapped into her late husband&nbsp;
Théo’s contacts in the art world

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:37.840
to approach art dealers, galleries, and&nbsp;
critics, seeking opportunities for sales,

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.000
exhibitions, and publicity&nbsp;
for Vincent’s paintings.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.320
She gave museums discounts on works&nbsp;
they were interested in acquiring,

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:49.080
or even donated them, recognizing that&nbsp;
this would increase public exposure.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:52.520
It also aligned with her belief that&nbsp;
art should be accessible to everyone,

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:56.040
not just the privileged,&nbsp;
reflecting her socialist values.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.080
She organized exhibitions in&nbsp;
Paris, The Hague, Antwerp,

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.680
Amsterdam, Berlin, and later London and New York.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.640
She managed everything: coordinating with shipping&nbsp;
companies for the careful transport of Vincent’s

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:14.760
paintings, handling payments for florists, and&nbsp;
overseeing the provision of coffee, to name a few.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:19.160
She thanked her artist friends who helped&nbsp;
her out by generously gifting them van Goghs.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:22.680
This not only showed her appreciation&nbsp;
for their support but also ensured that

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.280
Vincent's work was appreciated&nbsp;
within the broader art community.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.200
Vincent's reputation began to build 15 years after

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:35.960
his death with a pivotal exhibition at&nbsp;
Amsterdam's Stedelijk Museum in 1905.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:41.160
No known photos exist of the exhibition exist so&nbsp;
we used AI and a bit of our own editing to imagine

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:48.320
what it looked like as 484 paintings and drawings&nbsp;
were displayed, the largest van Gogh exhibit ever.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.280
Jo smartly asked a number of&nbsp;
people who owned van Goghs to

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.880
lend them for the exhibition&nbsp;
as proof of his popularity.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.080
Her son Vincent helped arrange and hang the&nbsp;
paintings while her second husband Johan,

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:02.320
an artist, stayed in touch with potential buyers.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:05.400
When the public first saw van&nbsp;
Gogh’s art, some were shocked

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.520
by the bold colors and dramatic brush strokes.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:13.320
An art critic wrote: “...daring to&nbsp;
exhibit such shoddy work is scandalous.”

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.400
He suggested The Starry&nbsp;
Night looked like doughnuts.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.120
Whereas another art critic took the opposite view,

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:23.320
writing, “The work will grip&nbsp;
you and never let you go.”

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:28.600
Love him or hate him, they talked about him&nbsp;
which drove up the price of Vincent’s art.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:32.720
Gone were the days of selling a van Gogh&nbsp;
painting like the Interior of a Restaurant

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:39.200
for only 180 francs or the equivalent of around&nbsp;
$35 USD based on the gold standard of the time.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:44.080
After the exhibition, a Parisian gallery purchased&nbsp;
the painting The Bedroom, which depicts van

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:50.240
Gogh’s own bedroom, for 3,000 Dutch guilders or&nbsp;
around $1,205.88 USD, whereas five years before,

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:55.840
Jo turned down an offer for the same painting&nbsp;
of only 500 guilders, or $200 dollars.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:59.240
She believed it was better to sell&nbsp;
a few works for good prices than

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:03.800
more works for low prices in order&nbsp;
to preserve the value of the art.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.760
She also always made sure not to&nbsp;
flood the market with van Goghs,

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:11.040
understanding that scarcity&nbsp;
could increase their value.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.880
The surge in van Gogh’s popularity was&nbsp;
further fueled by a new generation of

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:20.120
collectors who were increasingly drawn to&nbsp;
the expressive qualities of modern art.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:24.800
Despite her success, Jo faced another&nbsp;
tragedy when her second husband, Johan,

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:30.960
died of pleurisy, an inflammation of the&nbsp;
lining of the lungs, in 1912 at the age of 38.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:37.320
Jo lamented, “Here I am, alone again, and the only&nbsp;
thing that sustains me and gives me the courage to

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:44.000
continue is the love for my dear son and the duty&nbsp;
to look after the interests of Vincent’s work.”

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.560
She buried herself deeper into her work,

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:50.480
including publishing all of Vincent’s&nbsp;
letters so that the public could get to

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:55.920
know him as a human being, hoping it would&nbsp;
foster a deeper appreciation for his art.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:01.920
She organized, edited, and arranged the&nbsp;
820 surviving letters sent by Vincent,

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:07.560
658 of which were to his best friend&nbsp;
and most loyal supporter, Théo.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.480
The letters were all the more special because

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.400
they contained sketches to give Théo&nbsp;
an idea of what he was working on.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.960
Théo treasured and preserved&nbsp;
his letters from Vincent.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.920
When they were published in 1914, first in Dutch,

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:23.440
readers were struck by the&nbsp;
intensity of Vincent’s writing.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:27.120
“Success is sometimes the outcome&nbsp;
of a whole string of failures.”

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:32.320
“Find things beautiful as much as you can,&nbsp;
most people find too little beautiful.”

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.560
“The sight of the stars always makes me dream.”

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:38.240
Jo often wept as she pored through the letters.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:42.680
She worked tirelessly to translate them into&nbsp;
English, drawing on her earlier experience

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:48.080
working as an English teacher, and also her time&nbsp;
spent living in New York during World War I.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:53.680
Even as Parkinson's disease caused her hands to&nbsp;
tremble, she persisted in her translation work,

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:56.880
eventually relying on others to dictate letters.

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:00.400
She managed to translate two-thirds&nbsp;
of the letters into English before

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:06.440
she passed away peacefully in&nbsp;
her sleep on September 2, 1925.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:11.720
As she neared the end of her life, she could&nbsp;
see the fruits of her lifelong dedication;

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:18.320
Vincent's art, once confined to an attic, now&nbsp;
graced museums in Europe and the United States.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.120
Among them were masterpieces she&nbsp;
had promised to hold onto forever,

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:25.120
yet made the difficult decision to let them go.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:29.240
When she sold one of the beloved 'Sunflower'&nbsp;
paintings to the National Gallery in London,

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:35.720
she explained to the museum director, “It is&nbsp;
a sacrifice for the sake of Vincent’s glory.”

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:40.160
Reflecting on the dramatic change in the&nbsp;
perception of van Gogh’s work, Jo confided

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:45.960
to an art critic: “It is so good, after so many&nbsp;
years of public indifference, even hostility

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:52.960
towards Vincent and his work, to feel towards&nbsp;
the end of my life that the battle is won.”

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:57.040
Following Jo’s death, her son Vincent&nbsp;
took over the van Gogh collection

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:01.200
and was dedicated to ensuring it would&nbsp;
stay accessible for future generations.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:04.360
He helped establish the Vincent&nbsp;
van Gogh Foundation leading to

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:07.880
the creation of the Van Gogh Museum in Amsterdam.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:09.920
Vincent van Gogh once wrote:

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.920
“It’s a rather sad prospect to&nbsp;
have to say to myself that the

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:16.440
painting I do will perhaps never have any value.”

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:22.240
How wrong he was. If only he could 'see' the&nbsp;
admiration the world holds for him today.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:28.440
Dr. Who, the time traveler doctor&nbsp;
brings van Gogh to the future,

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:31.320
where he’s taking in an exhibit of his own work.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:35.640
To meet, van Gogh is the finest&nbsp;
painter in the world. Certainly,

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:40.520
the most popular, great painter of all time.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:47.440
The value of his legacy is not measured in mere&nbsp;
emotional impact but also in astounding prices.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:55.160
In 2022, Orchard with Cypresses was purchased&nbsp;
at Christie’s auction house for $117.2 million,

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:58.160
the most expensive van Gogh painting ever sold.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.480
Underscoring the profound influence&nbsp;
he continues to have on the art world.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:07.600
An influence that Jo van Gogh-Bonger&nbsp;
spent 35 years cultivating.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:13.720
As one headline aptly put it, “Without&nbsp;
Jo, there would have been no van Gogh.

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:16.680
Did you know that The Starry Night&nbsp;
actually mirrors a scientific

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:22.760
concept not fully grasped until years&nbsp;
after its creation: turbulent flow.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:26.040
Van Gogh’s ability to visually&nbsp;
represent this phenomenon,

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:31.040
possibly intuitively, is a testament to&nbsp;
his deep observation of the natural world.

